"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_electrical_engineering_en"][30]={"university_electrical_engineering_en:M30-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M30-L1 – Entropy and Mutual Information: What a Signal Reveals about Its Source<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Master’s Specialisation: Information Theory and Optical Communication<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate entropy, conditional\nentropy and mutual information, and distinguish between them.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Information as a measure of surprise<\/h2>\n<p>Information theory asks how many independent decisions a message\ncontains statistically. The meaning of a text to a human reader is not\nmeasured by this quantity. For a symbol\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>x<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x)&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i(x)=-\\log_2 p(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis its self-information in bits. A certain event yields zero bits,\nwhereas an event with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields three bits. The self-information of independent events adds up\nbecause their probabilities multiply.<\/p>\n<p>The mean self-information of a discrete random variable is the\nShannon entropy<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/munder><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(X)=-\\sum_x p(x)\\log_2 p(x).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The convention\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\log_2 0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\navoids an undefined boundary term. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>m<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\npossible symbols,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>m<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\le H(X)\\le\\log_2 m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe maximum requires a uniform distribution. An individual symbol need\nnot be representable in exactly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(X)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbits: entropy is a mean and becomes an asymptotic rate only over many\nsymbols.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000247_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Binary entropy function and decomposition of uncertainty for a binary symmetric channel.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Binary entropy rises to a maximum for equal symbol\nprobabilities and falls thereafter; remaining mutual information for a\nbinary symmetric channel is shown separately.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Joint and conditional entropy<\/h2>\n<p>Joint entropy uses the joint distribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x,y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFrom\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x,y)=p(y)p(x|y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe chain rule follows:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><\/munder><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(X,Y)=H(Y)+H(X|Y),\\qquad H(X|Y)=\\sum_y p(y)H(X|Y=y).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>If a receiver knows\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe mean remaining uncertainty about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay be smaller. For independent variables,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(X|Y)=H(X)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a deterministically invertible relationship, this uncertainty\nvanishes. For a uniformly distributed bit\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nthat is inverted with probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis also uniformly distributed. Using the binary entropy function<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_2(p)=-p\\log_2 p-(1-p)\\log_2(1-p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>we obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.469<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(X|Y)=h_2(0.1)=0.469<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbits and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.469<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(X,Y)=1.469<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbits. This channel model is used in L3 to study capacity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Mutual information and divergence<\/h2>\n<p>The mean information that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Y<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Y<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nprovides about\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>X<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mfrac><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mfrac><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I(X;Y)=H(X)-H(X|Y)=\\sum_{x,y}p(x,y)\\log_2\\frac{p(x,y)}{p(x)p(y)}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The formula is symmetric. It corresponds to the Kullback–Leibler\ndivergence between the joint distribution and the product of the\nmarginal distributions. In general,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">‖<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D(P\\Vert Q)=\\sum_x P(x)\\log_2[P(x)/Q(x)]\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(potentially infinite if support is missing), but it is not a symmetric\nmetric. In the binary example,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.531<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0.531<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbits per channel use. An incorrectly detected bit is not equivalent to\nlosing an entire bit of information: error probability and mean\nuncertainty are distinct quantities.<\/p>\n<p>For a Markov chain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo>→<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\to Y\\to Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I(X;Z)\\le I(X;Y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA hard threshold decision can therefore discard information that a\nsubsequent decoder could have used with soft values.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Estimation from data<\/h2>\n<p>For a measured sequence of length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe relative frequencies\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>p<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">̂<\/mo><\/mover><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>n<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\hat p_i=n_i/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan be substituted into the entropy formula. A sample with 800 zeros and\n200 ones yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.722<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">h_2(0.2)=0.722<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbits per symbol. For finite samples, the plug-in estimate is usually\ndownward-biased; rare unobserved events are missing. Temporal dependence\nadditionally requires analysing the joint distribution of several\nsymbols. An alternating sequence, for example, has a marginal entropy of\nabout 1 bit, but if the initial phase is known it contains no new\ninformation per subsequent symbol. The entropy rate of a stationary\nsource is the limit of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(X_1,\\ldots,X_n)/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nprovided it exists. Histogram entropy alone is therefore not a reliable\npromise of compression.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and review<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>H<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor four equally probable symbols. <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 2\nbits/symbol.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For a binary symmetric channel, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(X|Y)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a uniform input and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">A channel always inverts every bit. <strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(X|Y)=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a uniform input; a known inversion can be completely reversed.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=124625&amp;ansicht=3&amp;lang=de&amp;seite=1&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZurich – Communication (Curriculum G6)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-441-information-theory-spring-2010/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOCW – 6.441 Information Theory, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M30-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M30-L2 – Source Coding, Typical Sequences and Compression Limits<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Master’s Specialisation: Information Theory and Optical Communication<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M30-L1<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Construct prefix codes and\ndistinguish between mean codeword lengths and asymptotic compression\nlimits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Lossless representation<\/h2>\n<p>A lossless source code has a unique inverse mapping. For four symbols\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A,B,C,D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=(1/2,1/4,1/8,1/8)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na fixed-length code requires two bits per symbol. A prefix code, by\ncontrast, is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>110<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=110<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>111<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=111<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nNo codeword is a prefix of another. A bitstream can therefore be decoded\nfrom left to right without separators, provided the initial codeword\nboundary is known.<\/p>\n<p>The mean length\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mover><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar \\ell=\\sum_i p_i\\ell_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis 1.75 bits/symbol. The entropy is also 1.75 bits/symbol. Equality is\npossible here because the probabilities are negative integer powers of\ntwo. In the selected model, the code is 12.5% shorter than the\nfixed-length code. Transmission errors can, however, disrupt\nsynchronisation in variable-length codes.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000248_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Comparison of fixed-length coding, Huffman coding and entropy for a four-symbol source.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: For a non-uniform four-symbol source,\nvariable-length Huffman codewords reduce average length relative to\nfixed-length words; entropy provides the theoretical\nbenchmark.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Kraft inequality and the Huffman algorithm<\/h2>\n<p>For binary prefix codes,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i 2^{-\\ell_i}\\le1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe codewords can be imagined as leaves of a binary tree: a word of\nlength\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ℓ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\ell<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\noccupies the fraction\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mi>ℓ<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{-\\ell}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nof the tree. Conversely, a prefix code exists for integer lengths\nsatisfying this inequality. The Huffman algorithm repeatedly combines\nthe two smallest weights into a node. The branches are then labelled\nwith 0 and 1. It minimises the mean length for symbol-by-symbol binary\nprefix codes with known probabilities.<\/p>\n<p>For a discrete memoryless source, in the non-trivial case,<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>ℓ<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">u<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(X)\\le\\bar\\ell_{\\mathrm{Huffman}}&lt;H(X)+1.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The gap of up to nearly one bit per symbol is large for highly skewed\nbinary sources. Block coding of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>k<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent symbols reduces the additive gap to less than\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbits per original symbol, but requires up to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>m<\/mi><mi>k<\/mi><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m^k<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncode leaves.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Typical sequences and the compression limit<\/h2>\n<p>For independent, identically distributed symbols, the law of large\nnumbers also applies to the self-information of the sequence. The\nasymptotic equipartition property (AEP) states that<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mi>n<\/mi><\/mfrac><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>→<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\frac1n\\log_2 p(X_1,\\ldots,X_n)\\longrightarrow H(X)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>in probability. A typical set contains sequences whose normalised\nself-information differs from the entropy by at most\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>ϵ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts probability tends to one, and its size asymptotically lies between\napproximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{n(H-\\epsilon)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mrow><mi>n<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>H<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2^{n(H+\\epsilon)}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(with the lower bound additionally multiplied by the probability mass of\nthe typical set). For 100 Bernoulli symbols with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.25<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.8113<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H\\approx0.8113<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbits per symbol. The dominant region can be described asymptotically by\napproximately 81 bits per 100 source bits; this is not a guarantee for\nany single block. Rare sequences require a fallback representation if\nthe code is to be strictly lossless. A fixed-length code allowing a\nvanishing block error probability is a different problem from an\nalways-lossless variable-length code.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Compression in a transmission system<\/h2>\n<p>A continuous sensor producing 10,000 symbols/s with the four-symbol\nsource above yields an average Huffman payload of 17.5 kbit/s instead of\n20 kbit/s. A codebook, block headers, integrity checks and\nsynchronisation require additional bits. A source with memory can be\nexploited more effectively through prediction or context-dependent\nmodelling. For audio and image data, lossy coding permits additional\nreduction if a distortion measure and tolerable quality are defined. The\nentropy of a quantised signal is not equivalent to the differential\nentropy of the analogue signal. L3 discusses the deliberate introduction\nof redundancy for error correction: compression removes statistical\nredundancy, whereas channel coding adds controlled redundancy.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and review<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Check the lengths\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,2,3,3)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> Kraft sum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2+1/4+1/8+1/8=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Encode\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>A<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><mi>C<\/mi><mi>D<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ABCD<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> <code>010110111<\/code>, nine bits (not the\nexpected value of seven bits for four random symbols).<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">For blocks of eight symbols, what is the maximum additive Huffman\ngap? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> less than 0.125 bits per symbol for\nindependent symbols.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=124625&amp;ansicht=3&amp;lang=de&amp;seite=1&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZurich – Communication (Curriculum G6)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-441-information-theory-spring-2010/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOCW – 6.441 Information Theory, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M30-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M30-L3 – Channel Capacity, Channel Coding and the Difference between Information Rate and Raw Bit Rate<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Master’s Specialisation: Information Theory and Optical Communication<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M30-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M30-L2<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Determine capacity per channel\nuse and per second, and understand the limitations of finite codes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From the transition law to capacity<\/h2>\n<p>A discrete memoryless channel is specified by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(y|x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor successive independent channel disturbances, the block transition\nprobability factorises. Capacity is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/munder><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=\\max_{p(x)} I(X;Y)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>in bits per channel use, possibly subject to constraints on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor the binary symmetric channel (BSC) with inversion probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na uniform input maximises mutual information:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(Y)=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhile\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>H<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Y<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H(Y|X)=h_2(p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis independent of the input distribution. Consequently,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>h<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=1-h_2(p)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.01<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.01<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthis yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.9192<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=0.9192<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nonly\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mn>0.5310<\/mn><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.5310<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbits/use. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p&gt;0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nan inverting receiver makes sense; a known complete inversion has\ncapacity one.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000249_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Capacity of the binary symmetric channel and illustrative payload data rates of a coded link.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: As bit-flip probability increases, binary symmetric\nchannel capacity initially decreases; the illustrated net information\nrates additionally account for a selected code rate.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Continuous Gaussian channel<\/h2>\n<p>For an ideal bandlimited AWGN channel with bandwidth\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>B<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin Hz, mean signal power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>P<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand noise power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>N<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">N<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the band under consideration, the usual Shannon model assumptions\ngive<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>B<\/mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=B\\log_2(1+P/N).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>B<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B=10<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nMHz and an SNR of 15 dB\n(<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>N<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>31.62<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P/N=31.62<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>),\nthe capacity is approximately 50.28 Mbit/s. This formula allows\ncontinuous Gaussian-distributed signalling under an average power\nconstraint. A fixed discrete modulation alphabet and a hard decision\ndevice do not automatically achieve the formula. An optical intensity\nchannel additionally has non-negative intensity, optical power limits\nand potentially signal-dependent noise. The AWGN formula cannot then be\napplied directly to optical power without further modelling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Channel coding and finite block length<\/h2>\n<p>The channel coding theorem guarantees, at a rate below capacity, the\nexistence of codes whose error probability tends to zero as the block\nlength increases. It promises neither an immediately available code for\nevery rate below capacity nor a specific latency or BER value. A simple\nexample is the triple repetition code with majority decision.\nIndependent BSC errors give<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>p<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_3=3p^2(1-p)+p^3.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe error probability falls from 0.1 to 0.028, but three channel uses\ncarry only one payload bit. A block code\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>n<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(n,k)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas code rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>k<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_c=k/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nits minimum Hamming distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d_{\\min}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nguarantees correction of up to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⌊<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⌋<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\lfloor(d_{\\min}-1)/2\\rfloor<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nerrors with suitable decoding. FEC methods such as LDPC use considerably\nlonger blocks and often soft decisions. The theoretical existence of\nreliable coding must be distinguished from measured decoder\nperformance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Raw bit rate, net rate and requirements<\/h2>\n<p>For symbol rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na modulation alphabet with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>M<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>2<\/mn><mi>m<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">M=2^m<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsymbols and code rate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\na simple fixed mapping gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mi>M<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_{\\mathrm{roh}}=R_s\\log_2 M,\\qquad R_{\\mathrm{netto}}=R_{\\mathrm{roh}}R_c\\eta_{\\mathrm{frame}}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The subscripts <code>roh<\/code> and <code>netto<\/code> denote the raw\nand net rates, respectively.\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>η<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">f<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta_{\\mathrm{frame}}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\naccounts for pilot and header overhead, for example. For 10 GBd QPSK,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_c=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>η<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.95<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\eta=0.95<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe result is 20 Gbit/s raw and 15.2 Gbit/s net for one polarisation.\nCapacity must match the channel definition and the number of independent\ndegrees of freedom used. For 10 GBd BPSK or OOK with one bit/symbol, by\ncontrast, the raw bit rate is 10 Gbit/s. The optical case study uses\nuncoded bits; a target BER of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a didactic comparison value, not proof of FEC performance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and review<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">BSC with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2781<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=0.2781<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbits/use. A reliable rate of 0.5 is asymptotically impossible in this\nmodel.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Repetition with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.02<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=0.02<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>:\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.001184<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_3=0.001184<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat rate 1/3.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">25 GBd, 16-QAM,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R<\/mi><mi>c<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.75<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R_c=0.75<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nframe factor 0.9: <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 100 Gbit/s raw, 67.5 Gbit/s\nnet (one polarisation).<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=124625&amp;ansicht=3&amp;lang=de&amp;seite=1&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZurich – Communication (Curriculum G6)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-441-information-theory-spring-2010/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOCW – 6.441 Information Theory, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M30-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M30-L4 – Optical Transmission Chains: Fibre Attenuation, Dispersion and Receiver Noise<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Master’s Specialisation: Information Theory and Optical Communication<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L3<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M19&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M19-L5<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M30-L3<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Calculate power and dispersion\nbudgets separately and understand photodetector noise.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Optical chain and power<\/h2>\n<p>A transmitter modulates an optical carrier, and a fibre guides it to\nthe receiver. In the receiver path, attenuation, temporal distortion and\nnoise affect the decision. Optical power in dBm is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>10<\/mn><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>10<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">W<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_{\\mathrm{dBm}}=10\\log_{10}(P/1\\,\\mathrm{mW})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor an average transmit power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_T=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndBm, fixed losses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">A_0=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndB and a fibre attenuation coefficient\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>α<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha=0{.}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndB/km, the teaching model gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>A<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mi>α<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_R[\\mathrm{dBm}]=P_T-A_0-\\alpha L.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At 85 km this yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndBm or 10 µW, and at 105 km it yields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>24<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-24<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndBm or 3.981 µW. These values are synthetic assumptions for an\nunamplified link, not the specifications of a particular fibre. In\nreal-world planning, a margin for ageing, connectors and additional\nlosses must be stated separately. A 2 dB margin reduces the reach\ndetermined solely by this linear power limit by 10 km.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000250_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Received power and chromatic delay spread of a modelled optical fibre link.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Received power on the modelled fibre route decreases\nwith distance while chromatic delay spread grows; both limits affect\nusable reach.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Chromatic dispersion<\/h2>\n<p>Frequency-dependent group delay broadens optical pulses. For a small\nspectral width, the delay spread can be roughly estimated as<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t\\approx |D|L\\Delta\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>D<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin ps/(nm km),\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>λ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta\\lambda<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin nm and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin km. With\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>17<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D=17<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nps/(nm km) and an illustrative effective spectral width of 0.025 nm, the\nspread at 85 km is 36.125 ps. At 10 GBd,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>100<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T_s=100<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nps. A separate screening criterion for the intensity receiver without\nequalisation is chosen as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t\\le T_s/2=50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nps. This corresponds to a maximum of 117.65 km. It is not a universal\nBER limit; pulse shape, chirp, the definition of width and receiver\nfiltering determine the actual effect on the eye diagram. The spectrum\nis taken as given in this calculation, rather than derived from a\nparticular modulator pulse. The coherent model path assumes idealised\ndispersion equalisation. It is therefore not automatically free of real\ndispersion limits.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Photodetector and noise contributions<\/h2>\n<p>For a photodetector with responsivity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℛ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin A/W,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>I<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℛ<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I=\\mathcal R P<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℛ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal R=0{.}8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nA/W and 10 µW average power, the average photocurrent is 8 µA. In the\nsimple Poisson model, the one-sided shot-noise current variance is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\mathrm{shot}}^2=2q_e(I+I_d)B_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the dark current and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">B_e<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the effective electrical noise bandwidth. An ohmic resistor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>R<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat temperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>T<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nyields\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>σ<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">h<\/mi><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><msub><mi>k<\/mi><mi>B<\/mi><\/msub><mi>T<\/mi><msub><mi>B<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma_{\\mathrm{th}}^2=4k_BT B_e/R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the ideal current-noise model. At 300 K, 50 Ω and 5 GHz, thermal\nnoise is approximately 1.29 µA RMS, and shot noise (at 8 µA, with no\ndark current) is about 0.113 µA RMS. A real TIA has frequency-dependent\ncurrent- and voltage-noise contributions; this simple circuit is merely\nan order-of-magnitude example and does not calibrate the L6 study.<\/p>\n<p>Laser intensity noise, amplifier ASE, polarisation, nonlinear effects\nand receiver saturation can introduce additional contributions.\nIndependent noise sources add in variance, not by summing their RMS\namplitudes.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Limitations and verification strategy<\/h2>\n<p>A power budget alone does not determine the bit error rate. The\nmapping from received power to decision statistics must also be known. A\ntwo-stage comparison first examines the noise channel and then checks\nwhether the idealised decision conditions (such as negligible\nintersymbol interference) are plausible. L5 introduces two explicitly\nsynthetic receiver characteristics. A realistic extension requires\nmeasured or specified noise parameters, actual modulated waveforms, the\nfibre transfer function, sampling and, where needed, equalisation.\nOptical signal-to-noise ratios (OSNR) are stated with a specified\noptical reference bandwidth; they cannot be interchanged with electrical\nSNR or\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E_b/N_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwithout conversion.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and review<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nat 95 km? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> −22 dBm or 6.31 µW.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What delay spread occurs at 130 km? <strong>Solution:<\/strong> 55.25\nps; OOK fails the chosen 50 ps screening criterion.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">With the same power and half the symbol duration:\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> the relative dispersion\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>T<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t/T_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndoubles; the electrical noise bandwidth may also increase.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=124625&amp;ansicht=3&amp;lang=de&amp;seite=1&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZurich – Communication (Curriculum G6)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.inue.uni-stuttgart.de/teaching/OpticalComms/\">University\nof Stuttgart – Optical Communications, course<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itu.int/epublications/publication/itu-t-g-suppl-39-2025-03-optical-system-design-and-engineering-considerations/en\">ITU-T\nG Supplement 39 (03/2025) – Optical system design and engineering\nconsiderations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M30-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M30-L5 – System Comparison: Direct Detection, Coherent Reception and Presentation of Results<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Master’s Specialisation: Information Theory and Optical Communication<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M30-L3<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M30-L4<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M25&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M25-L4<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish intensity and field\nmeasurements and derive two simplified BER characteristics.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Intensity versus field<\/h2>\n<p>In direct intensity detection, a photodiode generates a current\nproportional to optical power, i.e. to the square of the field\nmagnitude. A simple OOK (on-off keying) signal switches between two\npower levels. An intensity measurement alone cannot distinguish two\nfields of equal magnitude and opposite phase. In coherent detection, the\nreceived field is optically mixed with a local oscillator. The\ndifference between two photocurrents in an ideal balanced mixer contains\na cross term proportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msqrt><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mrow><mi>L<\/mi><mi>O<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">cos<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{P_RP_{LO}}\\cos\\phi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA 90-degree hybrid and multiple balanced detectors enable I and Q\nmeasurements. BPSK uses two opposite phases. QPSK and QAM use multiple\nstates; for a fair comparison at the same raw bit rate, the case study\nconsiders only binary BPSK with an ideal phase reference.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000251_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Direct detection and coherent reception with separate modelled decision paths.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Direct detection evaluates optical intensity;\ncoherent reception mixes the field with a local oscillator, making phase\navailable for subsequent decisions.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Gaussian threshold decision<\/h2>\n<p>For equiprobable bits and the symmetric decision variable\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>b<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z|b\\sim\\mathcal N((2b-1)q,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nzero is the optimal threshold. The probability of error follows directly\nfrom the standard normal distribution:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">erfc<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mfrac><mi>q<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><\/mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_e=Q(q)=\\frac12\\operatorname{erfc}\\left(\\frac{q}{\\sqrt2}\\right).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>For OOK with two current levels\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_0,I_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nsubject to equal noise variance,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>σ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=(I_1-I_0)/(2\\sigma)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor ideal extinction,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℛ<\/mi><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">I_1=2\\mathcal R P_R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(equiprobable bits, average power), so the Q value is proportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_R<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nif\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>σ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis constant. With signal-dependent noise or unequal variances, the\noptimal threshold generally differs. For ideal coherent BPSK in an AWGN\nchannel,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=\\sqrt{2E_b/N_0}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the BER is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><msub><mi>N<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(\\sqrt{2E_b/N_0})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn the strong-LO limiting model with a fixed LO and approximately\nconstant noise level, the useful signal current is proportional to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{P_R}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThese are separate regimes, not a universal ranking of performance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A transparently calibrated teaching model<\/h2>\n<p>At the reference power\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>20<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_0=-20<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndBm, we set the decision values\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_{\\mathrm{OOK},0}=3{.}5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_{\\mathrm{BPSK},0}=4{.}2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThey are freely chosen synthetic calibration values, neither\nmeasurements nor manufacturer sensitivity specifications. With the power\nratio\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m<\/mi><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=10^{(P_R[\\mathrm{dBm}]+20)/10}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><mi>r<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mi>r<\/mi><\/msqrt><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_{\\mathrm{OOK}}=3{.}5r,\\qquad q_{\\mathrm{BPSK}}=4{.}2\\sqrt r.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>At −20 dBm this gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>326<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_e\\approx2{.}326\\cdot10^{-4}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>335<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1{.}335\\cdot10^{-5}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrespectively. At −22 dBm the ratio is 0.631, hence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>208<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=2{.}208<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand 3.336 respectively. The BPSK characteristic is flatter at lower\npowers. At −16 dBm, by contrast, OOK has the larger Q value (8.792\nrather than 6.657). The comparison specifically does not prove that\ncoherent receivers are better under all conditions. Equal average\nreceived power, equal symbol rate and equal raw bit rate form the basis\nof comparison here; LO energy, DSP effort, laser linewidth, costs and\npower consumption are not included.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Presentation of results and decision<\/h2>\n<p>BER curves are usually plotted on a logarithmic scale against\nreceived power or distance. Zero observed errors do not establish BER=0;\nwith no errors in\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>n<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nindependent bit decisions, the exact one-sided 95% upper bound is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><msup><mn>05<\/mn><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>n<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1-0{.}05^{1/n}\\approx3/n<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA comparison should specify both receivers and their assumptions, raw\nbit rate, target BER and separate distortion criteria. The L6 study uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nas an uncoded comparison target and first makes a decision based on the\ntheoretical BER. A real system selection must additionally consider the\ndelivered payload rate, costs, power demand, availability, operational\nrisks and implemented error correction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and review<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">What BER results from\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><mn>35<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1{.}35\\cdot10^{-3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Halving optical power: <strong>Solution:<\/strong> OOK Q is halved,\nand BPSK Q decreases by a factor of\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msqrt><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sqrt{1/2}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin the chosen model.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Why does the coherent variant require phase recovery?\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> an unknown phase rotation shifts the decision\naxis; without a reference, even a low-noise signal may be decoded\nincorrectly.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=124625&amp;ansicht=3&amp;lang=de&amp;seite=1&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZurich – Communication (Curriculum G6)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.inue.uni-stuttgart.de/teaching/OpticalComms/\">University\nof Stuttgart – Optical Communications, course<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itu.int/epublications/publication/itu-t-g-suppl-39-2025-03-optical-system-design-and-engineering-considerations/en\">ITU-T\nG Supplement 39 (03/2025) – Optical system design and engineering\nconsiderations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-441-information-theory-spring-2010/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOCW – 6.441 Information Theory, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_electrical_engineering_en:M30-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M30-L6 – Specialisation Project: Evaluating the Bit Error Rate and Reach of a Modelled Optical Link<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M30 – Master’s Specialisation: Information Theory and Optical Communication<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Master’s Specialisation<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M30-L1<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M30-L2<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M30-L3<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M30-L4<\/a>, <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_electrical-engineering&amp;module=M30&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M30-L5<\/a>.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Conduct a reproducible\ncomparison of two optical decision models and document the limits of\ntheir applicability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Project brief and acceptance criteria<\/h2>\n<p>A virtual unamplified fibre link is to be compared at 10 GBd (10\nGbit/s gross, binary, uncoded, one polarisation). Variant A uses OOK\nwith direct detection; variant B uses idealised coherent BPSK reception.\nThe theoretical BER must not exceed\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">10^{-3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\na separate dispersion screening criterion,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>50<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\Delta t\\le50<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nps, also applies to OOK. Ideal equalisation and phase recovery are\nassumed for BPSK. This is a symbol-by-symbol decision model without a\ntime-domain fibre waveform, not a physical link simulator.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Model parameter<\/th>\n<th>Value<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Average launch power<\/td>\n<td>0 dBm<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fixed losses<\/td>\n<td>3 dB<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fibre attenuation<\/td>\n<td>0.2 dB/km<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dispersion parameter / effective width<\/td>\n<td>17 ps/(nm km) / 0.025 nm<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reference power<\/td>\n<td>−20 dBm<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Q at reference power<\/td>\n<td>3.5 OOK / 4.2 BPSK<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Symbol rate<\/td>\n<td>10 GBd<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bit decisions per case<\/td>\n<td>2,000,000<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Random number generator seed<\/td>\n<td>3006<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>The values are chosen for teaching purposes. Neither a specific\noptical fibre nor a particular receiver product is specified. Using the\nsame reference power permits comparison of the model characteristics,\nnot of the actual power consumption of the receivers.<\/p>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-electrical-engineering-en/media/img/000252_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Theoretical BER curves and Monte Carlo points with reach and dispersion criteria.\" />\n<figcaption>Figure: Theoretical and simulated bit-error rates are\ncompared against fibre length; acceptance of the modelled link also\ndepends on receiver-power and dispersion limits.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Calculation procedure and independent reference<\/h2>\n<p>For each distance\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>L<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ncalculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_R=-3-0.2L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(dBm),\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>r<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><mn>20<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>10<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">r=10^{(P_R+20)/10}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the Q values from L5. The random number generator produces\nequiprobable bits and independent normalised Gaussian noise. An error\noccurs when the threshold decision differs from the original bit. Theory\nserves as the reference:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>e<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">erfc<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mn>2<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_e=\\operatorname{erfc}(q/\\sqrt2)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIn addition, the normal distribution tail is integrated numerically\nusing the trapezoidal rule and compared with the closed-form expression.\nThe stochastic check uses a generous limit of six standard deviations\nplus five errors; it is not a confidence interval construction and does\nnot establish the realism of the Gaussian model.<\/p>\n<p>The code requires Python and NumPy (at least NumPy 2.0 for\n<code>trapezoid<\/code>). The printed German label\n<code>BER-Grenze km<\/code> means BER-limited reach in kilometres.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> erfc, sqrt, log10<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>N <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">2_000_000<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>LENGTHS <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">65<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">85<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">95<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">105<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">115<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">130<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> theory(q):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.5<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> erfc(q <span class=\"op\">/<\/span> sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> simulate():<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    rng <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">3006<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    rows <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> []<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> length <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> LENGTHS:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-11\"><a href=\"#cb1-11\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        pr <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span> <span class=\"op\">-<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.2<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> length<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-12\"><a href=\"#cb1-12\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        ratio <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span>((pr <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">20<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-13\"><a href=\"#cb1-13\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        spread <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">17<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.025<\/span> <span class=\"op\">*<\/span> length<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-14\"><a href=\"#cb1-14\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> mode, q <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [(<span class=\"st\">&#39;OOK&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">3.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>ratio), (<span class=\"st\">&#39;BPSK&#39;<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">4.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>sqrt(ratio))]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-15\"><a href=\"#cb1-15\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            bits <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> rng.integers(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>, <span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>, N, dtype<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.int8)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-16\"><a href=\"#cb1-16\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            y <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>bits<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>q <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> rng.standard_normal(N)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-17\"><a href=\"#cb1-17\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            errors <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">int<\/span>(np.count_nonzero((y <span class=\"op\">&gt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>) <span class=\"op\">!=<\/span> bits))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-18\"><a href=\"#cb1-18\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            p <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> theory(q)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-19\"><a href=\"#cb1-19\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"co\"># Conservative statistical check, not a confidence interval.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-20\"><a href=\"#cb1-20\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(errors<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>N<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>p) <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">6<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>sqrt(N<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>p<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p))<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-21\"><a href=\"#cb1-21\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"co\"># Independent numerical integration of normal tail (trapezoids).<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-22\"><a href=\"#cb1-22\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            x <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.linspace(q, <span class=\"bu\">max<\/span>(<span class=\"fl\">12.<\/span>, q<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>), <span class=\"dv\">40001<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-23\"><a href=\"#cb1-23\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            integral <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> np.trapezoid(np.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>x<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi), x)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-24\"><a href=\"#cb1-24\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(integral<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>p) <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-8<\/span> <span class=\"op\">+<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>p<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-25\"><a href=\"#cb1-25\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            rows.append((length, mode, pr, spread, q, errors, p))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-26\"><a href=\"#cb1-26\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> rows<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-27\"><a href=\"#cb1-27\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> <span class=\"va\">__name__<\/span> <span class=\"op\">==<\/span> <span class=\"st\">&#39;__main__&#39;<\/span>:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-28\"><a href=\"#cb1-28\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> l,m,pr,s,q,e,p <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> simulate():<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-29\"><a href=\"#cb1-29\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"ss\">f&#39;<\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>l<span class=\"sc\">:3}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>m<span class=\"sc\">:4}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>pr<span class=\"sc\">:6.1f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>s<span class=\"sc\">:6.2f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>q<span class=\"sc\">:6.3f}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>e<span class=\"sc\">:7}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>e<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>N<span class=\"sc\">:.5g}<\/span><span class=\"ss\"> <\/span><span class=\"sc\">{<\/span>p<span class=\"sc\">:.5g}<\/span><span class=\"ss\">&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-30\"><a href=\"#cb1-30\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> mode, q0, exponent <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> [(<span class=\"st\">&#39;OOK&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">3.5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>),(<span class=\"st\">&#39;BPSK&#39;<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">4.2<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>)]:<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-31\"><a href=\"#cb1-31\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        lo, hi <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">0.<\/span>, <span class=\"fl\">200.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-32\"><a href=\"#cb1-32\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">60<\/span>):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-33\"><a href=\"#cb1-33\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            mid <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> (lo<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>hi)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-34\"><a href=\"#cb1-34\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            q <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> q0<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span><span class=\"op\">**<\/span>(exponent<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">17<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>mid)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">10<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-35\"><a href=\"#cb1-35\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> theory(q) <span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">1e-3<\/span>: lo <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> mid<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-36\"><a href=\"#cb1-36\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>            <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span>: hi <span class=\"op\">=<\/span> mid<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-37\"><a href=\"#cb1-37\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>        <span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(mode, <span class=\"st\">&#39;BER-Grenze km:&#39;<\/span>, lo)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Results of the executed model calculation<\/h2>\n<table>\n<colgroup>\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-12\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-12\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-12\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-12\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-12\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-12\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-12\">\n<col class=\"lc-col-width-12\">\n<\/colgroup>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Length (km)<\/th>\n<th>Mode<\/th>\n<th>Received power (dBm)<\/th>\n<th>Spread (ps)<\/th>\n<th>q<\/th>\n<th>Errors / 2 million<\/th>\n<th>Sample BER<\/th>\n<th>Theoretical BER<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>65<\/td>\n<td>OOK<\/td>\n<td>-16.0<\/td>\n<td>27.63<\/td>\n<td>8.792<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>7.3721e-19<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>65<\/td>\n<td>BPSK<\/td>\n<td>-16.0<\/td>\n<td>27.63<\/td>\n<td>6.657<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>0<\/td>\n<td>1.4016e-11<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>85<\/td>\n<td>OOK<\/td>\n<td>-20.0<\/td>\n<td>36.13<\/td>\n<td>3.500<\/td>\n<td>476<\/td>\n<td>0.000238<\/td>\n<td>0.00023263<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>85<\/td>\n<td>BPSK<\/td>\n<td>-20.0<\/td>\n<td>36.13<\/td>\n<td>4.200<\/td>\n<td>30<\/td>\n<td>1.5e-05<\/td>\n<td>1.3346e-05<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>95<\/td>\n<td>OOK<\/td>\n<td>-22.0<\/td>\n<td>40.38<\/td>\n<td>2.208<\/td>\n<td>27356<\/td>\n<td>0.013678<\/td>\n<td>0.01361<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>95<\/td>\n<td>BPSK<\/td>\n<td>-22.0<\/td>\n<td>40.38<\/td>\n<td>3.336<\/td>\n<td>881<\/td>\n<td>0.0004405<\/td>\n<td>0.00042469<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>105<\/td>\n<td>OOK<\/td>\n<td>-24.0<\/td>\n<td>44.63<\/td>\n<td>1.393<\/td>\n<td>164075<\/td>\n<td>0.082037<\/td>\n<td>0.081753<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>105<\/td>\n<td>BPSK<\/td>\n<td>-24.0<\/td>\n<td>44.63<\/td>\n<td>2.650<\/td>\n<td>7871<\/td>\n<td>0.0039355<\/td>\n<td>0.0040243<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>115<\/td>\n<td>OOK<\/td>\n<td>-26.0<\/td>\n<td>48.88<\/td>\n<td>0.879<\/td>\n<td>378933<\/td>\n<td>0.18947<\/td>\n<td>0.18966<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>115<\/td>\n<td>BPSK<\/td>\n<td>-26.0<\/td>\n<td>48.88<\/td>\n<td>2.105<\/td>\n<td>35805<\/td>\n<td>0.017902<\/td>\n<td>0.017646<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>130<\/td>\n<td>OOK<\/td>\n<td>-29.0<\/td>\n<td>55.25<\/td>\n<td>0.441<\/td>\n<td>659996<\/td>\n<td>0.33<\/td>\n<td>0.32974<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>130<\/td>\n<td>BPSK<\/td>\n<td>-29.0<\/td>\n<td>55.25<\/td>\n<td>1.490<\/td>\n<td>136542<\/td>\n<td>0.068271<\/td>\n<td>0.068084<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p>All twelve cases satisfy the statistical comparison and the numerical\nintegral check. At 65 km, neither sample contains errors; this implies\nonly a one-sided 95% upper bound of approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1.50<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1.50\\cdot10^{-6}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor each sample. Extremely small theoretical BER values from the\nGaussian assumption cannot be confirmed directly with two million bits.\nAt 85 km, both cases meet the theoretical BER target, and OOK meets the\nspread criterion. At 95 km, only BPSK meets the BER requirement. At 105\nand 115 km, neither meets the BER requirement; at 130 km, the OOK\ndispersion criterion is also violated. All simulated BER values are\nnoise-only results; in particular, the OOK point at 130 km must not be\ninterpreted as a complete transmission prediction.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Reach, sensitivity and further validation<\/h2>\n<p>From\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mn>10<\/mn><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Q(q_*)=10^{-3}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nit follows that\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.0902<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_*\\approx3.0902<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe power limit follows from the monotonic Q characteristic; bisection\nin the code gives 87.704 km for OOK and 98.326 km for BPSK. The separate\nOOK screening gives 117.647 km, so noise is the limiting factor first in\nthis model. An additional fixed 2 dB loss margin reduces both\npower-limited reaches by 10 km. If the effective spectral width of the\nOOK model is doubled to 0.05 nm, the dispersion limit falls to 58.824\nkm; the screening then becomes the stricter condition. The BPSK value\nstill refers to ideally compensated dispersion and does not constitute a\ngeneral reach advantage of 10.6 km.<\/p>\n<p>A further investigation should define a pulse shape from which the\nactual spectral width follows. The frequency-dependent fibre phase,\nreceiver filters, sampling instant and, where appropriate, adaptive\nequalisation should then be simulated. Several independent seeds and\nsuitable confidence intervals should quantify rare errors. For FEC, the\ncode, decoder, frame structure, pre- and post-FEC errors and net rate\nmust be documented separately. No real optical measurement or hardware\nvalidation is available.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercises and checks<\/h2>\n<ol type=\"1\" class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">1.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Add 90 km. <strong>Solution for checking:<\/strong>\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>P<\/mi><mi>R<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>21<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P_R=-21<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndBm,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>2.780<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_{\\mathrm{OOK}}\\approx2.780<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">S<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.743<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_{\\mathrm{BPSK}}\\approx3.743<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nonly BPSK meets the BER requirement.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">2.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Determine the spread at 85 km for twice the effective width.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> 72.25 ps, screening violated; the noise-only\nBER remains unchanged in the simplified code and must not be interpreted\nas the total BER.<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">3.<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\">Design a results summary for a real experiment.\n<strong>Solution:<\/strong> record at least fibre type, wavelength,\npower, temperature, losses, modulation rate, filters, DSP/FEC\nconfiguration, number of measured bits and errors, uncertainty and\nreference measurement.<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2 id=\"S6\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.vvz.ethz.ch/Vorlesungsverzeichnis/sucheLehrangebot.view?abschnittId=124625&amp;ansicht=3&amp;lang=de&amp;seite=1&amp;semkez=2026W\">ETH\nZürich – Communication (Curriculum G6)<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/6-441-information-theory-spring-2010/pages/lecture-notes/\">MIT\nOCW – 6.441 Information Theory, Lecture Notes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.inue.uni-stuttgart.de/teaching/OpticalComms/\">University\nof Stuttgart – Optical Communications, course<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://www.itu.int/epublications/publication/itu-t-g-suppl-39-2025-03-optical-system-design-and-engineering-considerations/en\">ITU-T\nG Supplement 39 (03/2025) – Optical system design and engineering\nconsiderations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a><\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
